From: Proton CRM <noreply@proton.local>
Subject: [PATCH] one place to set your status, not two that disagree

The sidebar's "Set your availability" and the "My status" page answered the
same question with different answers. This menu offered Chatwoot's fixed three
(Online/Busy/Offline); My status offered the operator-defined catalogue. An
agent who picked "Offsite Visit" on one page saw "Busy" here and reasonably
concluded something was broken.

They are not actually two systems, and the backend already proves it:
`status_router.py`'s `set_status` PATCHes Chatwoot's native availability FIRST
and only appends the presence event once that succeeds. Choosing a custom
status already moves the native one underneath it -- so this menu can offer the
catalogue and nothing needs to write both.

WHAT STAYS NATIVE, and why it is not an oversight:

  * OFFLINE. The backend refuses it (`SYSTEM_MANAGED_KEYS` -- "Offline is
    Chatwoot's own availability control"), so routing it through the Proton
    endpoint would 400. It is appended to the custom list rather than dropped,
    because dropping it would leave an agent no way to go offline from this
    menu at all.
  * AFTER-CALL WORK, filtered out with the other `system_managed` entries: it
    is entered at call end and times out on its own. Offering it as a choice
    would let an agent enter a state the system is supposed to own.

FAIL-OPEN, the same posture as every other Proton surface: catalogue empty,
feature off, or the request failed -> `customStatuses` stays empty and this
menu renders exactly the three items it always did, byte-identical. That also
covers today's state on a tenant that has not defined any statuses yet.

After a successful set, the native value is dispatched to Vuex. The backend
already wrote it to Chatwoot, but this SPA's store does not know that, and the
store is what renders the dot here and on every avatar. It rewrites the value
Chatwoot already holds, so it is idempotent -- and there is no action that
merely re-reads availability (`getCurrentUserAvailability` is a getter, not an
action; dispatching it would have failed silently).

